  "scriptId": "age-04-linked-family-age-relations",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Linked Family Ratios and Age Chains",
    "description": "Connect several age relationships without losing which person each fraction, ratio, or age gap belongs to.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with a concrete age situation before the equation, label every past/present/future date clearly, show the reason for each age shift, and check the result in the original clue. After each worked example, give a fresh near-transfer question. Wrong answers receive a misconception-specific correction and a parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 31,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Teach linked age relationships across three or more people: convert statements such as one person being a fraction or multiple of another into one-variable expressions; combine pairwise ratios by matching the shared person; use a total, a difference, or one known age to set the scale; and carry a known age through a fraction chain. Include family chains with both multiplication and fixed year gaps, detect when ratios alone are insufficient, and finish with a combined-ratio problem followed by a future age shift. Keep every relation attached to the named person, and check all original clues."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Age clues often form a chain. One person is a fraction of another, who is a fraction of someone else. The trick is to make every link use the same unit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose Ravi is half of Meera’s age and one-third of his father’s age.\n\nLet Ravi = x.\nMeera = 2x.\nFather = 3x.\n\nSo the ratio Ravi : Meera : Father is 1 : 2 : 3.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Why start from Ravi? Both clues are written around him. One variable keeps the chain clean.\n\nAlways turn each sentence into an age expression first. Then compare the expressions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
